00:02:36.984 --> 00:02:38.970
And now back to the show.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.110
Since the show began,

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:40.980
Barry has descended

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.607
into ever-more-violent antics,

00:02:42.607 --> 00:02:44.850
from murdering a perfectly nice detective

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.063
to going on a needless rampage

00:02:46.063 --> 00:02:47.196
through a drug storehouse,

00:02:47.196 --> 00:02:48.630
to killing his clients

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.058
when they have a sudden change of heart.

00:02:50.058 --> 00:02:52.080
- There is no forgiving Jeff!

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:52.913
- Not exactly plot lines

00:02:52.913 --> 00:02:54.360
conducive to great comedy.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.440
To understand how Barry

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.412
keeps delivering the laughs,

00:02:56.412 --> 00:02:57.555
we need to look at a genre

00:02:57.555 --> 00:02:59.640
variously called "dark comedy,"

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:00.780
or "sick comedy,"

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.950
or "black comedy."

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:03.030
While humans have been laughing

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.140
since we were monkeys,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.310
dark comedy is something

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.600
of a newcomer in our species'

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.830
collective standup routine.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.277
The co-founder of
Surrealism, Andre Breton,

00:03:10.277 --> 00:03:11.554
coined the phrase

00:03:11.554 --> 00:03:13.020
"l'humour noir,"

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.600
i.e. black comedy, in 1939.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.770
He believed that dark comedy

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.660
had an inherently modern sensibility.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.010
Though it had existed in literature

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.660
and painting throughout the ages,

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.860
early dark humor mostly

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.693
took the form

00:03:23.693 --> 00:03:25.620
of didactic, moralizing satire.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.090
Modern dark humor, he thought,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.950
was more subversive and aggressive,

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.900
and devoid of moral "lessons."

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:31.980
Which kind of sounds a bit

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:32.880
like our favorite show

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.507
about a hitman you
could bring home to mom,

00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:36.390
but more on that in a moment.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.070
But what about in American culture?

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.210
When it comes to film,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.440
you can see dark humor

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.240
boiling up after World War II

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.890
in works like "Unfaithfully Yours"

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.120
and "Monsieur Verdoux."

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.687
As Wheeler Winston Dixon writes,

00:03:46.687 --> 00:03:48.967
"In postwar America, and the world,

00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:51.007
"the old social structures were crumbling,

00:03:51.007 --> 00:03:52.537
"to be replaced by an emptiness

00:03:52.537 --> 00:03:53.977
"which allowed greater transgression

00:03:53.977 --> 00:03:55.740
"against perceived normative rules."

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.306
You saw this in stand-up comedy,

00:03:57.306 --> 00:03:58.650
where "sick comedy"

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.120
like your favorite dead baby jokes

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.080
started popping up.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:01.980
But the genre really

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.600
came to a head in the '60s,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.740
once the studio system

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.240
had breathed its last gasp.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.016
As Dixon notes, "In the 1960s,

00:04:08.016 --> 00:04:09.487
"themes which had previously

00:04:09.487 --> 00:04:10.597
"been dealt with only in

00:04:10.597 --> 00:04:12.037
"the most serious fashion

00:04:12.037 --> 00:04:14.520
"were suddenly subject
to burlesque, or parody."

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.930
Dark humor dominated film

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.220
in the rebellious '60s,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.690
but mostly petered out of cinema

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:19.590
in the next decade.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.540
On TV, "consensus television,"

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.220
i.e. inoffensive shows that tried

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.180
to appeal to the widest

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.800
possible audience, dominated.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.090
Keeping dark comedy mostly

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:27.923
off the air

00:04:27.923 --> 00:04:29.130
for most of the medium's history.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.530
That's with the notable exception of MASH,

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.180
which used a war hospital setting

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.013
for dark humor

00:04:34.013 --> 00:04:36.210
and social critique of
American militarism.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.260
After MASH ended,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.580
mainstream '80s television

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.720
was basically absent

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.280
of any similarly-dark humor,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.660
although glimmers of dark comedy

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.860
emerged again in the '90s

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.230
in the form of popular
programs like Seinfeld.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.070
- You know what they call him?

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:48.043
Soup Nazi.

00:04:48.043 --> 00:04:48.876
- Shh.

00:04:48.876 --> 00:04:50.550
- The balance of
chipper-to-cynical programming

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.450
changed as we moved

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.860
into the 21st century,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.046
for two likely reasons.

00:04:54.046 --> 00:04:55.650
One, the rise of cable,

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:56.483
and later streaming,

00:04:56.483 --> 00:04:58.890
allowed for greater
creativity and innovation

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.120
as well as the opportunity

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.040
to appeal to more niche audiences,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.200
i.e. people with twisted senses of humor.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.995
And two, 9/11 and the collective

00:05:05.995 --> 00:05:07.500
trauma surrounding it.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.246
Much like the aftermath of World War II,

00:05:09.246 --> 00:05:11.100
this collective trauma inspired

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.540
a whole lot of dark comedy.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.580
Scholar Philip Scepanski argues that,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.897
when it comes to dark comedy post-9/11,

00:05:16.897 --> 00:05:18.937
"One of the genre's most significant roles

00:05:18.937 --> 00:05:19.987
"was as Novocaine.

00:05:19.987 --> 00:05:21.607
"By soothing the unpleasant feelings

00:05:21.607 --> 00:05:23.557
"associated with events like 9/11,

00:05:23.557 --> 00:05:24.757
"the sick humor of shows

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.257
"like Family Guy and South Park

00:05:26.257 --> 00:05:27.757
"calloused the public in such a way

00:05:27.757 --> 00:05:30.090
"that we could approach
policy more rationally."

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.740
Dark comedy, often political,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.096
dominated throughout the decade.

00:05:33.096 --> 00:05:35.820
But less-explicitly-political dark comedy

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.070
arguably exploded across the mainstream

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.320
in the 2000s and 2010s, too.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.550
Think It's Always Sunny,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:42.383
BoJack Horseman,

00:05:42.383 --> 00:05:43.860
Curb Your Enthusiasm,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.156
Baskets, The Good Place,

00:05:45.156 --> 00:05:48.000
Rick &amp; Morty, Archer, and so on.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.410
Journalist Rachel Aroesti

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.460
argues in The Guardian

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:51.916
that the rise of dark comedy television

00:05:51.916 --> 00:05:52.948
in the 2010s,

00:05:52.948 --> 00:05:55.410
and the increasingly surreal dark comedies

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:56.550
of the 2020s

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.780
owe a lot to the influence

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.430
of the weirdos at Adult Swim.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:00.390
And wouldn't you know,

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.028
we made a whole video just about that,

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.690
so check it out sometime!

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.010
The numerous shows that employ

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.060
dark comedy today

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.440
mainly appeal to millennials,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.390
who, gee, wouldn't you know,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.130
are more depressed, underpaid,

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.360
uncertain about their futures

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.166
and so on than previous generations.

00:06:14.166 --> 00:06:15.575
♪ Baby ♪

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:16.408
(screaming)

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:18.480
- So Barry, which debuted in 2018,

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.700
was the perfect show for its (beep) time.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.050
So now that we have context

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.000
for Barry's delightfully twisted humor,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.590
it's worth looking at some of the tools

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.030
it employs to make us laugh.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:27.960
Primary among them

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.947
is something humor scholars variously call

00:06:29.947 --> 00:06:32.850
"humorous incongruity" or
"script incompatibility."

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.620
And yes, once somebody broke up with me

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.360
citing "script incompatibility."

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.890
Humorous incongruity describes

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.330
an irreconcilable conflict

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.920
between two opposing things,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.230
whose sheer incompatibility
incites laughter.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.060
Importantly, incongruities generally

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:45.925
rely on some kind of

00:06:45.925 --> 00:06:47.550
transgression of norms.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.620
We see incongruity all over Barry,

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.600
starting with the main character himself.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.470
Barry is a hitman

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.060
who commits gruesome acts.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.830
But he's also weirdly innocent,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.060
all wide-eyed and earnest

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.140
about becoming an actor.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.220
He's the perfect version

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.667
of what J.L. Styan calls

00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:03.450
"the comic-pathetic hero" or antihero,

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.437
who is "capable of suggesting complexity

00:07:05.437 --> 00:07:06.727
"because he has implicitly

00:07:06.727 --> 00:07:08.700
"two or more sides towards the spectator."

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:09.533
He's a hitman

00:07:09.533 --> 00:07:11.520
and an aspiring, mediocre actor

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.960
and we respond to him differently

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:13.980
depending on his behavior

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.120
at any given moment.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.590
Pathetically, though desperate

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:18.150
to be a better actor on stage,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.830
he's actually a pretty convincing one

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:20.663
in his real life,

00:07:20.663 --> 00:07:21.960
managing to hide his identity

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.160
from everyone around him.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.607
Styan notes that the antihero

00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:26.827
"often tends to assume universal qualities

00:07:26.827 --> 00:07:28.027
"through the very individual

00:07:28.027 --> 00:07:29.377
"and contradictory details

00:07:29.377 --> 00:07:30.600
"that go to make him up."

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.100
We can all relate on some level

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.060
to wishing we could trade

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:33.893
one talent in

00:07:33.893 --> 00:07:35.100
for something more glamorous,

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:35.933
even if we've never

00:07:35.933 --> 00:07:37.320
buried a dead body on a hill.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.180
Because of his incongruity of character,

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.407
Styan explains, "We respond with sympathy

00:07:41.407 --> 00:07:42.840
"and criticism, both."

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:43.680
The other characters

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.030
are similarly layered.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:46.620
Sally is a talented actor

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.150
with a rich emotional life,

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:49.890
but she's also so self-absorbed

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:50.940
that it takes her forever

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:51.960
to notice that she's dating

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.790
a super-duper-violent man!

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.350
- Sally, you're dating a violent guy.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.210
- What?

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.860
- [Helen] Hank is a ruthless mobster,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.990
but he's also gentle, kooky, inept,

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:01.918
and devoted to Barry,

00:08:01.918 --> 00:08:03.180
which makes his attempts

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.400
to be intimidating
generally quite comedic.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.496
- It's me, it's Hank.

00:08:07.496 --> 00:08:08.715
- I know it's Hank.

00:08:08.715 --> 00:08:09.548
- I'm in a wig!

00:08:09.548 --> 00:08:10.381
- [Helen] When he's confronted

00:08:10.381 --> 00:08:11.214
by a murderous Barry,

00:08:11.214 --> 00:08:12.360
who deliberates on whether or not

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.193
to kill him,

00:08:13.193 --> 00:08:14.610
the supreme tension of the scene

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.290
is undercut by Hank vomiting

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:17.861
in relief when Barry spares him.

00:08:17.861 --> 00:08:20.730
(vomiting)

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:21.563
- Oh, my God.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:23.583
Oh, my God.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:25.493
I vomited.

00:08:25.493 --> 00:08:26.326
- Hank is really

00:08:26.326 --> 00:08:28.050
just the worst person to be a mobster,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.550
and the "worst person for the job"

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.560
is almost always the funniest character.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.330
Hank's not wrong when he laments that--

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.350
- Real talk?

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.810
I shouldn't be manager of crime syndicate.

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.760
I should be manager of hotel.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.720
Chain of hotels.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.000
- [Helen] Gene, too, is
full of incongruities.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.920
He's pompous, but pathetic.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.990
He genuinely cares about his students,

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.210
but briskly charges them for his advice.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.630
- Now, I bill my private classes

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.280
through another loan-out.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.210
So I'm just gonna go

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:55.200
and get that ledger.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.820
- [Helen] Maybe most hilariously,

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.470
he's a revered and honestly,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.970
pretty decent acting teacher

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:02.340
who cannot (beep) act to save his life.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.510
- Stella!

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.499
- [Helen] Except when it
really counts, of course.

00:09:05.499 --> 00:09:08.013
- He knows everything, Barry.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.033
He knows you killed Janice.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.223
I'm so sorry.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:17.913
Just, let's get in the car and go.

00:09:17.913 --> 00:09:20.370
- [Helen] And Fuches
is a total opportunist

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.620
and a user who also is devastated

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.550
to realize that Barry

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:24.720
no longer needs him.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.520
- [Hank] You're not even
on his mind anymore.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.350
You're not a blip on his radar.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.700
- [Helen] These characters all contain

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.110
some inner contradiction,

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:31.987
what Styan calls

00:09:31.987 --> 00:09:35.040
"an incongruity of the
solemn and the ridiculous."

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.330
What's more, because the show

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:37.290
doesn't encourage us

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:38.700
to judge any of them,

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.140
we're able to enjoy their hijinks

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:41.040
without pesky things

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.480
like morality getting in the way.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.577
That's in line with theater critic

00:09:43.577 --> 00:09:45.030
Walter Kerr's contention

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:46.507
that as a storyteller,

00:09:46.507 --> 00:09:48.007
"It is better to make a man

00:09:48.007 --> 00:09:49.080
"than to make a point."

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.400
But Barry peddles heavily

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.810
in incongruity even beyond

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.160
the characters themselves.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.240
The world of the show

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.073
is split between

00:09:55.073 --> 00:09:55.906
the self-centered ambitions

00:09:55.906 --> 00:09:57.750
of aspiring Hollywood actors

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.970
and the brutality of
the mobster underworld.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:01.207
And when those two worlds meet,

00:10:01.207 --> 00:10:02.700
there's ample opportunity

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.590
for moments of pure incongruity.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:05.940
Like when Barry's Marine friends

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.590
crash a party hosted by an actor

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.700
most known for playing

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.050
live-action Pinocchio.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.580
Both sides are rendered ridiculous

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.050
by their clashing perspectives.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:13.883
Then there's the way

00:10:13.883 --> 00:10:15.000
mob life is presented.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.320
As with Barry's character,

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:18.750
brutality is balanced with innocence.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:19.770
Like how Hank worries

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.570
about finding "the right" heroin table

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:22.530
for his stash house.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:24.180
- I'm seeing dining room tables,

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.710
I'm seeing coffee tables,

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:28.293
but not sure how to look
for table that I need.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:30.900
No, it's for heroine.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.370
- [Helen] Or how the mobsters all enjoy

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:33.990
Cristobal's self-help seminars.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.160
- [Hank] Cristobal, their leader,

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.990
is guru mensch of highest order.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.610
Like baby Tony Robbins.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:39.990
- [Helen] The incongruity of mobsters

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.210
collectively striving
to be more actualized,

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:43.279
when their day to day job involves

00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:45.120
murder and drug trafficking,

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.680
never fails to make us laugh.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.730
Like the time Barry agrees

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:48.690
to train Hank's army

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:49.590
to pay back his debt

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.420
and make them as deadly as he is.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.100
The innocent joy the men express

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:53.933
as they train.

00:10:53.933 --> 00:10:55.080
- Hey, boss.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:55.913
Did you see?

00:10:55.913 --> 00:10:57.390
- [Helen] Is countered
by our sickly knowledge

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.223
that their new skills

00:10:58.223 --> 00:10:59.790
can only be used lethally.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.410
Or take the time in acting class

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:02.520
when Barry confesses

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.320
the story of his first kill.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.610
On its own, this story

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.680
would simply be intense and disturbing.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.630
But, it's coupled with the pathetic antics

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.850
of his not-very-talented classmates,

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:12.690
who try to act out

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:13.523
what they imagine

00:11:13.523 --> 00:11:14.790
his experience might be.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:15.653
The incongruity between

00:11:15.653 --> 00:11:18.060
how they imagine his reaction to killing,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.277
that he felt immense guilt.

00:11:19.277 --> 00:11:21.029
- Oh, God!

00:11:21.029 --> 00:11:22.260
I killed someone!

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.000
- Is countered by the brutal reality

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.440
of how he really felt,

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.342
elated and ready to bro out.

00:11:27.342 --> 00:11:29.367
♪ Barry Berkman ♪

00:11:29.367 --> 00:11:31.110
- And the acting class' inability

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:32.160
to even conceive

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.750
of what wartime might be like

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:34.950
is a crucial point.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:36.510
Much of Barry suggests that,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.070
by sequestering our experience

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:39.303
with America's recent wars,

00:11:39.303 --> 00:11:40.770
we're forcing soldiers

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:42.240
to bear the psychological brunt

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.890
of the country's foreign policy

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:44.723
while the rest of us

00:11:44.723 --> 00:11:46.170
remain blissfully unaware.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.460
More on that in a moment.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.470
Now, notably, a common incongruity

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.480
in dark comedy is killing a character,

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.800
and promptly discarding

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.222
their body and/or narrative.

00:11:54.222 --> 00:11:56.190
They're never to be heard from again.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.510
The ramifications of their death

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:58.860
go completely ignored.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.510
Here, the classic incongruity

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:01.830
is between the seriousness

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:03.630
of a human life being taken away,

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.130
and the nonchalance with which

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.480
that deadly act is treated.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.560
Think Brad Pitt's death

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:08.527
in the Coen Brothers film

00:12:08.527 --> 00:12:09.600
"Burn After Reading."

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.130
Barry sometimes follows this rule

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.690
during the first two seasons.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.430
There are exceptions, though.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.263
Like when a father

00:12:15.263 --> 00:12:16.110
mourns his son's death

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.150
at the hands of Barry's mobster friends.

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:19.380
Or the way Mr. Cosineau

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.330
is plagued by the loss of Janice.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.510
- Canceled, why?

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.070
- Because Gene had a full on

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:28.320
nervous breakdown yesterday and left.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.610
- Still, we're often allowed

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:30.720
to watch Barry's antics

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:31.770
without thinking too hard

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:32.973
about the people he's harming.

00:12:32.973 --> 00:12:34.320
We're even spared having

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:35.940
to actually watch Janice die.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.121
But in the latest episodes,

00:12:37.121 --> 00:12:38.880
after Fuches outs Barry

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.250
as the killer to his
victims' various relatives,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:42.870
the show keeps us laser-focused

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.730
on the consequences of his actions,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:45.990
and the pain he's caused.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:47.940
Not just to those he mercilessly killed,

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:49.890
but to those who loved his victims.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.750
This is very unusual territory

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:52.740
for a dark comedy,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.150
and we think that might be why

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.290
some people have commented

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:56.250
that this season feels

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.660
less like a straight comedy.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:12:58.980
We'd argue that the show's ability

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.600
to be more humane to its victims

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.040
than your average dark comedy,

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:03.660
while continuing to deliver laughs,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:04.530
is actually proof

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:06.090
of its profound success.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:07.230
So what is Barry,

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.550
and any dark comedy,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:09.660
effectively doing through

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:10.800
its skewed use of humor?

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.330
A lot of things at once, actually.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:13.290
According to scholars

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.127
Chiara Bucaria and Luca Barra,

00:13:15.127 --> 00:13:16.477
"Controversial humor is a way

00:13:16.477 --> 00:13:17.677
"to directly expose

00:13:17.677 --> 00:13:18.997
"what happens in the world,

00:13:18.997 --> 00:13:20.467
"to engage with the truth,

00:13:20.467 --> 00:13:21.877
"to confront and to respond

00:13:21.877 --> 00:13:24.360
"to a reality that is already in place."

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:25.920
But at the same time, they note,

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:27.277
to be funny, such humor

00:13:27.277 --> 00:13:29.467
"necessarily exaggerates, distorts,

00:13:29.467 --> 00:13:30.997
"and deforms such reality.

00:13:30.997 --> 00:13:32.617
"For example, through hyperbole,

00:13:32.617 --> 00:13:34.837
"irony, detachment, and emphasis.

00:13:34.837 --> 00:13:36.667
"Thus, highlighting the inauthenticity

00:13:36.667 --> 00:13:37.980
"behind representation."

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.090
We'd argue that Barry

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:40.920
is using its hyperbolic brutality

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.360
to critique the devaluation

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.880
of human life in post-9/11 America.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.380
This devaluation can be seen

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:47.404
both in wars abroad,

00:13:47.404 --> 00:13:48.840
where soldiers like Barry

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.142
have killed innocent civilians,

00:13:50.142 --> 00:13:52.080
and domestically, as it seems

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.580
like no amount of gun violence

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.020
will make American politicians

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:56.460
take meaningful action.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:57.600
The character of Barry,

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.340
a killer completely desensitized

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:01.170
towards violence while serving in Iraq,

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:02.670
is an avatar for our country's

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:03.900
larger desensitization

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.430
towards the real-world violence

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:06.270
America has wrought

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.320
in its war on terror,

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.000
and the violence that's far too common

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:09.990
within our borders.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.670
But is the violence we see in Barry

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.200
serving a larger purpose?

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.690
Scholar Kristin A. Murray would say yes,

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.467
arguing that "dark comedy emerged

00:14:17.467 --> 00:14:18.547
"from significant shifts

00:14:18.547 --> 00:14:19.897
"in people's relationship to,

00:14:19.897 --> 00:14:21.367
"and understanding of,

00:14:21.367 --> 00:14:23.700
"death in contemporary American society"

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:25.200
over the past half century.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:26.550
Because previous generations

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.900
contended with world wars,

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:28.920
incurable diseases,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.020
and generally shorter life expectancies,

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.670
death was a natural part of life.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.830
Today, we're far more
distanced from death,

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:36.840
in part due to higher life expectancies.

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:37.717
She argues that

00:14:37.717 --> 00:14:38.857
"this avoidance of death

00:14:38.857 --> 00:14:40.807
"is entrenched in US institutions,

00:14:40.807 --> 00:14:43.410
"cultural practices and
personal interactions"

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:44.520
in forms like the medical

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.870
and funeral industries,

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:47.220
where only trained professionals

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.500
actually confront death on a daily basis.

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.457
As a result, death has become increasingly

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:54.540
"medicalized, secularized and mediatized."

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.227
One consequence of this is that

00:14:56.227 --> 00:14:58.987
"death seems more
conceptual than practical;

00:14:58.987 --> 00:15:00.667
"it is like a concealed possibility

00:15:00.667 --> 00:15:02.040
"rather than a certainty."

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.030
At the same time,

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.144
Americans are more aware

00:15:04.144 --> 00:15:06.002
of more deaths than ever before,

00:15:06.002 --> 00:15:07.260
thanks to the ubiquity

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:08.190
of digital media.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.440
However, this kind of mediated death

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:11.940
is marked by what Murray calls

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:13.657
a "kind of disembodiment.

00:15:13.657 --> 00:15:14.977
"a crafted, marketed,

00:15:14.977 --> 00:15:16.897
"sanitized representation of loss

00:15:16.897 --> 00:15:17.977
"rather than a tangible,

00:15:17.977 --> 00:15:19.860
"traumatic physical demise."

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.000
As such, death becomes

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.380
this big mysterious thing

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:23.670
we don't understand,

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.140
but are constantly hearing about,

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:26.760
and that makes us effing nuts!

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:27.593
Murray argues that

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:29.647
"dark comedy is an urgent articulation

00:15:29.647 --> 00:15:31.147
"of the tension between visible

00:15:31.147 --> 00:15:32.940
"and hidden aspects of loss."

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:34.274
Scholar Paul Lewis would agree,

00:15:34.274 --> 00:15:36.937
writing that "the apparent intensification

00:15:36.937 --> 00:15:38.582
"of cruel humor in recent years

00:15:38.582 --> 00:15:41.400
"reveals a widely shared desire or need"

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:42.480
to better understand

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:44.130
and learn to cope with death.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:45.870
When we watch Barry off someone,

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:46.860
we're contending with those

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:47.820
realities of death

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:49.170
we don't typically encounter

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:50.040
in real life.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.210
And we'd argue that Barry

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:52.110
does a better job

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:53.580
of forcing us to confront death

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.710
than just about any show on the air,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:57.330
by finding darkly funny ways

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:58.882
to inject both drama and comedy

00:15:58.882 --> 00:16:00.220
into each individual death.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:02.070
- Looks like the Bolivians

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:02.947
made it here before we did.

00:16:02.947 --> 00:16:05.310
- Well now, you can't
rule other people out

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.143
just like that.

00:16:06.143 --> 00:16:07.020
- Now who's making work?

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:08.070
This was Bolivians.

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:09.180
- There's the incongruity

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:10.230
between the forgiveness

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:11.760
the two sworn enemies come to

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.170
in the third season premiere,

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:14.520
and Barry's deadly decision

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.020
not to show them any mercy.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.310
There's the tragic irony

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:18.240
of Ronny surviving

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:19.710
Barry's attack in season two

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:20.970
and killing the jealous cop

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:21.870
who put a hit on him,

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.090
only to be shot dead by police backup.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:25.380
And there's the mirroring effect

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:26.940
of two deaths in the finale.

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.950
One which we can see, but do not hear

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:31.401
and one which we can hear, but do not see.

00:16:31.401 --> 00:16:33.568
(roaring)

00:16:35.282 --> 00:16:37.532
(laughing)

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.050
Each one is forcing us

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:41.250
to imagine what someone's

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.480
final moments on earth

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:43.890
really look or sound like,

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:45.210
which is pretty metal.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:46.770
And what we fill in the blanks with

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.210
is probably more horrifying

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:49.170
than any reality

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:50.520
a camera could conjure.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:51.353
And of course,

00:16:51.353 --> 00:16:52.260
there's the constant refrain

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:53.190
of Barry declaring,

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:54.433
after yet another murder

00:16:54.433 --> 00:16:57.000
that he's going to stop being violent.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:57.833
- Starting now.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.094
- It becomes more darkly funny over time,

00:17:01.094 --> 00:17:02.820
as Barry returns to killing

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:04.740
like a sugar fiend to the Nutella jar.

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:05.573
As we mentioned,

00:17:05.573 --> 00:17:06.450
some argue that Barry

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:07.440
has become too bleak

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:08.850
to qualify as a comedy,

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:10.020
pointing out that the character

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.910
has become completely irredeemable.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:12.750
And that the carnage

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:13.830
he's inflicted on the world

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:14.820
is too excessive

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:16.380
to allow for laughter anymore.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.060
For us, this argument assumes

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:18.990
that Barry is trying

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.430
to make anything resembling

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:21.420
a moral point.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.190
But, in the vein of modern dark comedy,

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.480
it doesn't really seem

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:25.313
to be doing that.

00:17:25.313 --> 00:17:27.240
Rather it's, as Styan puts it,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:28.597
trying to "urge a truth about

00:17:28.597 --> 00:17:30.810
"the nature of people and things."

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:31.860
It's simply offering

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.260
an unflinching look at a real phenomenon.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.930
The contemporary
undervaluing of human life,

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:38.880
and not letting us look away for a moment.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.597
As Dixon writes, "Black comedy,

00:17:40.597 --> 00:17:41.437
"at its best,

00:17:41.437 --> 00:17:42.397
"permits the audience

00:17:42.397 --> 00:17:44.287
"not a single refuge or crutch.

00:17:44.287 --> 00:17:45.397
"Not even the art itself

00:17:45.397 --> 00:17:46.867
"is left available as a refuge

00:17:46.867 --> 00:17:47.977
"in which the reader may seek

00:17:47.977 --> 00:17:49.710
"aesthetic solace and meaning."

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.810
There's no comfort to be found in Barry,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.060
as every laugh is tinged with darkness.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.040
And that's what makes it so (beep) good.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:56.873
As we've said,

00:17:56.873 --> 00:17:58.290
dark comedy has long existed

00:17:58.290 --> 00:17:59.280
in various forms.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:00.210
But in modern days,

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:01.740
it seems to pop up during times

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.300
of political and social turbulence,

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:04.275
like the 1960s,

00:18:04.275 --> 00:18:06.060
or you know, right now.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:08.250
Barry unveils the violence of our times,

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:09.120
while also showing

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:10.080
how it's papered over

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.670
by mass media conglomerates

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:12.750
who feed us hackneyed,

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.100
dishonest narratives,

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.080
no matter how funny their premise may be.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:17.970
- But then she turns into stone.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:19.530
But then she's still horny,

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:22.800
so she sucks and then eats his dick.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.126
- It renders death vivid,

00:18:24.126 --> 00:18:26.070
in a time when our only exposure

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.090
to our own mortality

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:29.160
comes via the 24-hour news cycle.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:30.090
At the same time,

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:31.170
it forces us to reckon

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:33.030
with America's culture of violence.

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:34.110
All while being funny.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.550
To summarize a Socrates line

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:36.877
from Plato's Symposium,

00:18:36.877 --> 00:18:38.287
"Real poets use tragedy

00:18:38.287 --> 00:18:40.027
"and comedy both at once,

00:18:40.027 --> 00:18:41.617
"and human life is experienced

00:18:41.617 --> 00:18:43.470
"as a mix of the tragic and comedic."

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.090
We'd argue that makes Hader and Berg

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.340
nothing less than contemporary poets.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:48.750
But what do you guys think?

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.610
Does Barry straddle tragedy and comedy

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.170
while keeping its funny bone intact?

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:53.003
Or has the show gone

00:18:53.003 --> 00:18:54.360
too dark for chuckling?

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.440
Let us know in the comments.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:56.273
Thanks to our patrons

